# Build outputs
bin/
gridsim-client
gridsim-server
/hub
/orchestrator
/logs/
run_demo.txt
demo-pis.txt
dashboard.log
text_reader.txt

# Root-level binaries — arm64 cross-compiles, and anything a bare
# `go build ./cmd/x` or `./sim/x` drops in the working directory.
#
# THIS LIST WAS HAND-MAINTAINED AND INCOMPLETE, which is how a 30 MB mbapsdev
# ELF reached commit c7e4b84 through a `git add -A`. Enumerating a few names and
# trusting future edits to extend it is the same pattern that produced this
# repo's worst register-map defect one directory over; the list is now derived
# from what the tree can actually build (`ls -d cmd/*/ sim/*/`), which is a thing
# a reader can re-derive in one command rather than a set somebody remembered.
#
# Each entry is anchored with a leading slash so it hits the repo ROOT only —
# these names are also legitimate SOURCE directories (cmd/certify/, sim/modsim/)
# and an unanchored pattern would ignore the code as well as the binary.
/aggregator
/batsim
/certify
/client
/client-http
/conformance
/dashboard
/evsim
/gen-mbaps-certs
/gridsim
/gw-campaign
/gw-diff
/gw-mayhem
/httpsim
/mbapsdev
/metersim
/modsim
/modsim-client
/modsim-conformance
/mqttproxy
/nbdiag
/server
/server-http
/simapi
/southbound
/ssm-conformance
/tlsserver
/vtnsim

# Python virtual environment
gui/.venv/

# Private keys — never commit, ever.
# This catches server-key.pem, client-key.pem, vault/ca-key.pem, test keys, all of them.
*-key.pem

# Temporary client cert staging (SCP private key to device, then delete)
certs/client-staging/

# Test cert fixtures (regenerable via `make gen-test-certs`)
# NOTE: was "internal/tlsserver/testdata/certs/" (no such path exists — the
# server package lives at sim/tlsserver) until TASK-003 CI work found the typo
# had let 5 generated cert files get committed under sim/tlsserver/testdata/certs/.
sim/tlsserver/testdata/certs/
internal/tlsclient/testdata/certs/

# Golden files DO get committed — they're part of the test suite
!sim/tlsserver/testdata/golden/

# Bench replay artifacts (per-run, regenerated each replay)
replay-checkpoint.json
replay-ticklog-*.csv

# Mayhem QA run reports (per-run, regenerated each suite run)
qa-mayhem-*.md

# Go test artifacts
*.test
*.out

# Editor cruft
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~

# python
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# go.work: TASK-024 (AD-003(d)/(e)) retires the committed go.work in favor of
# proto.pin + vendor/lexa-proto/. Developers may still keep an untracked
# local go.work (`go work init . ../lexa-proto`) for cross-repo dev against a
# live lexa-proto checkout — it must never be committed again.
go.work
go.work.sum
.claude/worktrees/

# Dashboard V2 UI: dist/ IS committed (pure-go CI embeds it, see
# cmd/dashboard/main.go + Makefile `ui` target); node_modules never is.
cmd/dashboard/ui/node_modules
/vtnsim

# TLS key-log output. wolfSSL built with --enable-keylog-export ALSO writes its
# own ./sslkeylog.log in the process CWD (WOLFSSL_SSLKEYLOGFILE_OUTPUT is a
# compile-time constant, not the SSLKEYLOGFILE env var), independent of the
# bench's own keylog path in internal/wolfssl/keylog.go. Session secrets must
# never be committed.
sslkeylog.log
*.keylog

# Conformance evidence bundles (certify -out). These contain packet captures AND
# keys.log — real TLS session secrets exported by the keylog build. Bundles are
# run artifacts to be archived deliberately, never committed.
runs/
# stray root-level build output from `go build ./cmd/certify`
/certify

# Bench cert BACKUPS. certs/mbaps/ is the tracked fixture set (public certs
# committed, *-key.pem held out by the rule above); a dated backup of it is a
# local operational artifact of a bench reconfiguration and is not fixture
# material anyone builds against.
#
# The rule exists because commit 2a9752b swept one in — 19 public certs from a
# 2026-08-07 eth-split backup — through a `git add -A`. No private key reached
# the index, and that was the *-key.pem rule doing its job rather than anything
# the commit got right: the SAME directory holds 16 key files on disk. A backup
# whose keys happened to be named differently would have gone in with them.
certs/*.bak-*/
certs/*.backup*/
